        "q2": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Does the 48-inch barrier rule apply to a new pool in Lake Nona or Horizon West?",
            "answer": "<p>New residential pools in Florida fall under the Residential Swimming Pool Safety Act. The barrier rule is at least 48 inches high on the outside, with gates that close on their own and open away from the pool. The 46-inch tempered panel in two spigots gives that 48 inches. Lake Nona permits go through the City of Orlando; Horizon West goes through Orange County. Both confirm the barrier details at permit time.</p>",
            "pages": ""
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Will the hardware rust in Orlando's humidity without salt air?",
            "answer": "<p>No. The spigots, connectors, hinges and latches are 2205 marine-grade stainless steel, chosen for salt air on the coasts. Inland humidity and daily summer rain are easy conditions for it. The hardware carries a 10-year no-rust promise in both finishes. Well water from sprinklers can leave iron stains on any surface, so rinse the base of the spigots if your irrigation hits the fence.</p>",
            "pages": ""
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Who issues the permit: the City of Orlando or Orange County?",
            "answer": "<p>It depends on the address. Inside the city limits, including Lake Nona, downtown and College Park, the City of Orlando Permitting Services handles it. Dr. Phillips and Horizon West are unincorporated Orange County, which goes through the county's Division of Building Safety. Winter Park, Windermere, Maitland and Ocoee are separate cities with their own offices. Check your address on the county property appraiser's site, then call that office before you order.</p>",
