        "t1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Lanais, lakes and new neighborhoods</strong></p><p>Orlando sits far from both coasts, so there is no salt in the air. There is a pool behind many houses, though, and a screened lanai over it. In Dr. Phillips and Windermere, on the Butler Chain of Lakes, the pool deck looks over the water. A glass pool fence keeps the lake in view from the kitchen. Winter Park's chain of lakes, from Lake Virginia to Lake Maitland, has older homes with second-floor balconies and raised terraces. College Park, Baldwin Park and Thornton Park add bungalows and townhomes close to downtown.</p><p>The growth is in the southeast and southwest. Lake Nona's new neighborhoods and Horizon West's master-planned villages are full of two-story houses with open staircases. A glass stair railing is often part of the plan from day one. Summer brings humidity and an afternoon thunderstorm most days, which matters more for scheduling the install than for the materials.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Orange County rules and the pool act</strong></p><p>Orlando is outside the High-Velocity Hurricane Zone. Orange County is a wind-borne debris region under the Florida Building Code, 8th edition (2023), and the building department reviews how a railing is attached. Inside the city limits, which include Lake Nona, permits go through City of Orlando Permitting Services. Dr. Phillips and Horizon West are unincorporated and go through the Orange County Division of Building Safety. Winter Park and Windermere have their own offices.</p><p>For a house, the residential code sets a 36-inch guard on a balcony, deck or landing more than 30 inches above grade. Apartments and condos need 42 inches. New residential pools fall under the Residential Swimming Pool Safety Act. It sets a barrier at least 48 inches on the outside, with gates that close on their own and open away from the pool. The 46-inch panel gives that height. See our <a href=\"/pages/florida-building-code-for-glass-railings\">Florida building code page</a>, then confirm with your building department.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Delivery to Orlando and the DIY install</strong></p><p>Standard panels are processed in 2 to 3 business days and arrive in 5 to 10 business days by freight carrier with a liftgate. The crate comes down to your driveway, so have a helper ready to carry the panels through the side gate to the lanai. Custom sizes take 2 to 3 weeks in transit after processing. Orders over 3,000 dollars ship free, and the checkout price is final, with no duties, tariffs or hidden fees.</p><p>Installation is DIY. Each panel sits in two spigots fixed to the concrete pool deck or the wood deck, with screws sold separately. The <a href=\"/pages/glass-railing-installation-guide\">installation guide</a> and videos cover every step. Most standard jobs take about 4 hours, so a morning start beats the afternoon storm. Check the glass within 3 days of delivery and before installation; damage reported in that window is replaced or refunded at no cost.</p>"
          }
        }
